function fs_new_content_guides_3() {
    return [
    [
        'slug'    => 'social-security-2027-cola-increase',
        'title'   => 'Social Security 2027 COLA: 3.6% Raise and What It Means',
        'keyword' => 'social security cola 2027',
        'desc'    => 'Social Security benefits rise 3.6% in 2027. See when it arrives, what it means for your check, how it is calculated, and what Medicare and tax can take back.',
        'excerpt' => 'The Social Security Administration announced a 3.6% cost-of-living adjustment for 2027. Here is what it means for your check.',
        'content' => '<p class="lead">On October 14, 2026, the Social Security Administration announced a <strong>3.6% cost-of-living adjustment (COLA) for 2027</strong>. For the average retired worker the SSA estimates that is about <strong>$75 more a month</strong>. This guide explains what changes, when the new amount arrives, how the raise is calculated, and what can shrink it. You can see your own number in our <a href="{tool:social-security-cola-calculator}">Social Security COLA calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>2027 COLA announcement date</h2><p>The Social Security Administration announced the 2027 COLA on <strong>October 14, 2026</strong>, after the September inflation data was released. Forecasts before the announcement ranged from about 3.2% to 3.6% (the Senior Citizens League estimated about 3.6%, and AARP gave a range of 3.2% to 3.6%), so the final 3.6% landed at the top of those forecasts.</p>'
            . '<h2>The 2027 COLA at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>2027</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cost-of-living adjustment</td><td>3.6% (announced October 14, 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Average raise, retired worker</td><td>About $75 a month (SSA estimate)</td></tr><tr><td>Social Security payments with the new amount</td><td>Begin January 2027</td></tr><tr><td>SSI increase</td><td>Starts with the payment on December 31, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum earnings taxed for Social Security</td><td>$192,300 (up from $184,500 in 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings limit under full retirement age</td><td>$25,560 a year</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings limit in the year you reach full retirement age</td><td>$67,920</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>The raise applies to Social Security retirement, survivor and disability benefits and to Supplemental Security Income (SSI). The SSA says nearly 72 million Social Security beneficiaries and about 7.3 million SSI recipients are affected.</p>'
            . '<h2>What 3.6% means for your check</h2><p>Multiply your monthly benefit by 1.036. Here are some examples before any deductions:</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Current monthly benefit</th><th>New monthly benefit</th><th>Raise per month</th><th>Raise per year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$900</td><td>$932.40</td><td>$32.40</td><td>$389</td></tr><tr><td>$1,200</td><td>$1,243.20</td><td>$43.20</td><td>$518</td></tr><tr><td>$1,500</td><td>$1,554.00</td><td>$54.00</td><td>$648</td></tr><tr><td>$2,000</td><td>$2,072.00</td><td>$72.00</td><td>$864</td></tr><tr><td>$2,500</td><td>$2,590.00</td><td>$90.00</td><td>$1,080</td></tr><tr><td>$3,000</td><td>$3,108.00</td><td>$108.00</td><td>$1,296</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Your own notice may differ by a few cents, because the SSA rounds each new benefit to the nearest dime. Use the <a href="{tool:social-security-cola-calculator}">COLA calculator</a> to enter your exact benefit.</p>'
            . '<h2>2027 COLA estimate vs forecast: was it close?</h2><p>Unofficial estimates of the COLA are built from the monthly CPI-W reports for July, August and September, so by the time the September report arrives the number is almost fixed. A 2027 COLA forecast earlier in the year is much less certain. This year the early forecasts were a little lower than the official result, which is why the SSA’s figure is the one to use.</p>'
            . '<h2>How the COLA is calculated</h2><p>By law, the COLA is based on the Consumer Price Index for Urban Wage Earners and Clerical Workers (CPI-W). The SSA compares the average CPI-W for the third quarter (July, August and September) of this year with the same quarter of last year. If prices rose, benefits rise by the same percentage, rounded to the nearest tenth of a percent. If there is no increase, there is no COLA. This year the comparison produced 3.6%.</p><p>For context, the last three adjustments were 3.2% for 2024, 2.5% for 2025 and 2.8% for 2026, so the 2027 raise is larger than any of them.</p>'
            . '<h2>COLA history</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>COLA</th><th>Year</th><th>COLA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2020</td><td>1.6%</td><td>2024</td><td>3.2%</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>1.3%</td><td>2025</td><td>2.5%</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>5.9%</td><td>2026</td><td>2.8%</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>8.7%</td><td>2027</td><td>3.6%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The raise tracks inflation, so it is large when prices jump and small when they do not. Over time, small raises add up: each COLA is applied to the already increased benefit, which is why even modest yearly increases matter over a long retirement.</p>'
            . '<h2>Couples and two benefits</h2><p>The COLA applies to each benefit separately. If two spouses each receive $1,800 a month, together they receive $3,600; after a 3.6% raise they receive $3,729.60 a month, which is $129.60 more. If one spouse dies, the survivor generally keeps the larger of the two benefits, and the same COLA percentage applies to it.</p>'
            . '<h2>Is the COLA enough?</h2><p>The COLA is tied to the CPI-W, which measures price changes for urban wage earners and clerical workers, not for older adults specifically. Some advocates argue that an index weighted toward older people’s spending, such as health care, would be more accurate, and the Bureau of Labor Statistics publishes an experimental index (CPI-E) for people 62 and older. Congress has not adopted it for the COLA. The practical advice is the same either way: compare the raise with your own cost increases, especially rent, insurance and prescriptions.</p>'
            . '<h2>When the raise arrives</h2><ul><li><strong>Social Security:</strong> the new amount begins with payments in January 2027. Because most benefits are paid a month behind, the increase for December shows up in the January payment.</li><li><strong>SSI:</strong> recipients see the increase earlier, starting with the payment on December 31, 2026.</li><li><strong>Notices:</strong> the SSA sends notices in December to people who are not enrolled in paperless notices. If you have a my Social Security account, you can view your new benefit amount there.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Why your net raise may be smaller</h2><h3>Medicare Part B premium</h3><p>Most beneficiaries have their Medicare Part B premium deducted from their Social Security payment. The 2027 premium is set separately by Medicare and is normally announced in the fall. If it rises, the extra amount comes out of your raise, so the net increase is smaller than 3.6%. The calculator has a box for the premium change so you can test it once the amount is known.</p><h3>Taxes on benefits</h3><p>Depending on your other income, up to 85% of your benefits can be subject to federal income tax. The income thresholds that trigger the tax are not adjusted for inflation, so as benefits rise more people have taxable benefits. If you have federal tax withheld from your payments, the withholding grows with the raise. Use the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> to see the effect on your return.</p><h3>Other deductions</h3><p>Medicare Advantage or drug plan premiums, and voluntary tax withholding, can also reduce the amount that reaches your bank account.</p>'
            . '<h2>If you are still working</h2><p>If you claim benefits before full retirement age and keep working, the SSA withholds $1 in benefits for every $2 you earn above $25,560 in 2027. In the year you reach full retirement age, the limit is $67,920 and the SSA withholds $1 for every $3 above it. Withheld benefits are not lost: the SSA recalculates your benefit upward at full retirement age to account for the months it withheld. People who earn above the Social Security wage base also pay the 6.2% Social Security tax only up to $192,300 in 2027.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to use the raise in your plan</h2><ol><li><strong>Check your notice</strong> in December or your online account to confirm the exact amount.</li><li><strong>Update your budget</strong> with the net amount after Medicare and tax. The <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a> helps.</li><li><strong>Remember that a COLA only keeps pace with prices.</strong> It is not a real increase in buying power unless your costs rise by less than 3.6%. Seniors often spend more on health care and housing, which can rise faster than the CPI-W.</li><li><strong>Plan the long term.</strong> If you are still saving, see how Social Security fits in your retirement number in our <a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">retirement planning guide</a>.</li><li><strong>Watch for scams.</strong> The SSA does not ask for payment or personal details by text or email to give you a raise.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>If you have not claimed benefits yet</h2><p>You do not have to be receiving benefits to get COLAs. If you are 62 or older and have not claimed, the cost-of-living adjustments since the year you turned 62 are included when your first benefit is calculated. That is one reason waiting to claim raises the payment: the benefit grows from both the delayed-retirement credits after full retirement age and the COLAs that accumulate along the way. Our <a href="{tool:retirement-savings-calculator}">retirement savings calculator</a> can show how much more you may need to save.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to read your notice</h2><p>Your COLA notice lists your benefit before the raise, the new gross benefit, the amount withheld for Medicare premiums and any tax withholding you chose, and the net amount you will receive. Compare the net amount with your last statement. If something looks wrong, contact the SSA directly through ssa.gov or its national phone number rather than through a link in an unexpected message.</p>'
            . '<h2>Medicare enrollment season</h2><p>Medicare’s open enrollment runs from October 15 to December 7. This is the time to review your drug plan or Medicare Advantage plan, because premiums and coverage change each year. Because premiums are deducted from your Social Security payment, a plan that costs less can effectively increase the part of your COLA you keep.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes and myths</h2><ul><li><strong>"Everyone gets $75."</strong> That is an average. Your raise is 3.6% of your own benefit.</li><li><strong>"The raise is added to my check in full."</strong> Medicare premiums and tax withholding come out first.</li><li><strong>"I have to apply for the COLA."</strong> You do not. It is applied automatically.</li><li><strong>"The wage base is the same as the COLA."</strong> The $192,300 maximum is a separate number that applies to workers’ taxable earnings.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal advice. Figures come from the Social Security Administration; check ssa.gov for the latest numbers.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'    => [
            [ 'What is the 2027 Social Security COLA?', 'The Social Security Administration announced a 3.6% cost-of-living adjustment for 2027 on October 14, 2026.' ],
            [ 'How much will the average retiree get?', 'The SSA estimates the average retired worker’s benefit will rise by about $75 a month. Your own increase is 3.6% of your benefit.' ],
            [ 'When will the 2027 increase start?', 'Social Security payments with the new amount begin in January 2027. SSI recipients see the increase with the payment on December 31, 2026.' ],
            [ 'Was the 2027 COLA forecast accurate?', 'Pre-announcement estimates ranged from about 3.2% to 3.6%, and the official COLA of 3.6% matched the top of that range.' ],
            [ 'How is the COLA calculated?', 'It is the percentage rise in the CPI-W from the third quarter of last year to the third quarter of this year, rounded to the nearest tenth of a percent.' ],
            [ 'Will Medicare take part of my raise?', 'If the Part B premium rises, the increase is usually deducted from your Social Security payment, so the net raise can be smaller than 3.6%.' ],
            [ 'Do I need to apply for the COLA?', 'No. It is added automatically, and the SSA sends a notice in December to people who are not paperless.' ],
            [ 'What is the 2027 COLA announcement date?', 'The SSA announced the 3.6% cost-of-living adjustment on October 14, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern.' ],
            [ 'Does the 2027 COLA apply to disability benefits?', 'Yes. The 3.6% applies to Social Security retirement, survivor and disability (SSDI) benefits and to SSI.' ],
            [ 'What is the 2027 maximum taxable earnings?', '$192,300, up from $184,500 in 2026. Social Security tax applies to earnings up to that amount.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'    => 'trump-accounts-explained',
        'title'   => 'Trump Account for Kids: Who Qualifies, Limits, How to Open',
        'keyword' => 'trump account for kids',
        'desc'    => 'The Trump account for kids explained: who gets the $1,000 federal deposit, limits, investments, withdrawal rules, how to open one and how it compares with a 529.',
        'excerpt' => 'A plain-English guide to Trump Accounts: eligibility, the $1,000 pilot deposit, limits, investments and how they compare with a 529 plan.',
        'content' => '<p class="lead">A Trump Account is a new kind of savings account for children, created by the 2025 tax law. Eligible children can receive a one-time $1,000 deposit from the federal government, and family members and employers can add more. This guide explains the rules published by the IRS and Treasury, how to open an account and how it compares with other ways to save for a child. You can test growth scenarios in our <a href="{tool:trump-account-calculator}">Trump Account calculator</a>.</p><p><em>Rules are still being finalized. The IRS has said regulations are coming, so check IRS.gov and trumpaccounts.gov for the latest details before you act.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>What is a Trump account for kids?</h2><p>The IRS describes a Trump Account as a new type of individual retirement account (IRA) for eligible children. An account is set up for a child under 18 by a parent or guardian, usually by making an election with the IRS. The money must be invested in a mutual fund or exchange-traded fund that tracks the S&amp;P 500 or another index of mostly U.S. stocks.</p>'
            . '<h2>Key facts at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who can have one?</td><td>A child who has not turned 18 by the end of the election year, with a Social Security number</td></tr><tr><td>Who gets the $1,000 federal deposit?</td><td>U.S. citizen children born January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028, once an election is made</td></tr><tr><td>Earliest contributions</td><td>July 4, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Yearly limit from family and others</td><td>$5,000 in total for 2026 and 2027, adjusted for inflation after 2027</td></tr><tr><td>Employer contributions</td><td>Up to $2,500 a year per employee, excluded from the employee’s income; counts toward the $5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Investments</td><td>Index funds that track the S&amp;P 500 or a similar U.S. stock index</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawals</td><td>Generally not before January 1 of the year the child turns 18; after that, treated generally like a traditional IRA</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>Who is eligible for a Trump account?</h2><p>Any child who has not turned 18 by the end of the election year and has a Social Security number can have an account. The extra $1,000 deposit is narrower: it is for U.S. citizen children born from 2025 through 2028. If you search for <em>Trump account eligibility</em>, those two tests, the account and the $1,000, are the part most people mix up.</p>'
            . '<h2>The $1,000 pilot deposit</h2><p>The government will make a one-time $1,000 contribution to a Trump Account for each eligible child who is a U.S. citizen, was born from 2025 through 2028, and has an election made for them. The child needs a Social Security number and must not already have a pilot election. The pilot deposit does not count toward the $5,000 yearly contribution limit. Children born outside those years can still have an account, but they do not receive the $1,000.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to open one</h2><ol><li><strong>Check eligibility.</strong> Confirm your child’s birth year, citizenship and Social Security number.</li><li><strong>Make the election.</strong> The IRS created Form 4547, "Trump Account Election(s)", which opens the account and requests the $1,000 pilot deposit. It can be filed with a federal tax return, and the program also provides an online election through trumpaccounts.gov. Follow the instructions on those official pages for the current process.</li><li><strong>Choose how to manage it.</strong> The account is held with an approved trustee and invested in the allowed index funds.</li><li><strong>Add contributions if you wish.</strong> Contributions can begin from July 4, 2026 and are limited as described below.</li></ol><p>A parent or guardian normally makes the election. The IRS instructions explain who else can act if a parent does not.</p>'
            . '<h2>Contribution limits</h2><ul><li><strong>$5,000 a year</strong> from family, friends and employers combined, for 2026 and 2027. It is indexed to inflation after 2027.</li><li><strong>$2,500 from an employer</strong> per employee each year under a written program. The amount is not taxable income for the employee and counts toward the $5,000.</li><li><strong>The $1,000 pilot deposit</strong>, certain qualified general contributions and rollovers do not count toward the $5,000.</li></ul><p>Some employers and philanthropists have announced plans to add money to children’s accounts. For example, Michael and Susan Dell have publicly pledged $250 for certain younger children who do not qualify for the federal $1,000. Details of such programs change, so check trumpaccounts.gov before relying on them.</p>'
            . '<h2>Withdrawals and taxes</h2><p>Under the IRS guidance, money generally cannot be withdrawn before January 1 of the calendar year in which the child turns 18. After that, the account is generally treated as a traditional IRA. That means IRA tax rules generally apply to withdrawals, including a possible tax on earnings and penalties for early withdrawals before retirement age. Because the regulations are not final, ask a tax professional about your situation.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump Account vs 529 vs other child accounts</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Trump Account</th><th>529 plan</th><th>Custodial (UTMA) account</th><th>Roth IRA for a child</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main purpose</td><td>Long-term savings, treated like an IRA</td><td>Education costs</td><td>Any use for the child</td><td>Retirement</td></tr><tr><td>Investments</td><td>Limited to U.S. stock index funds</td><td>Plan menu</td><td>Almost anything</td><td>Almost anything</td></tr><tr><td>Government seed money</td><td>$1,000 for children born 2025 to 2028</td><td>None federally</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Who owns the money</td><td>The child, managed by a guardian</td><td>The account owner</td><td>The child at adulthood</td><td>The child</td></tr><tr><td>Tax benefit</td><td>See IRS guidance</td><td>Tax-free growth for qualified education expenses</td><td>Limited (kiddie tax)</td><td>Tax-free growth if rules are met; needs earned income</td></tr></tbody></table><p>They can be used together. A 529 plan is designed for education costs, while a Trump Account is a long-term, IRA-style account. Our guide to <a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">index fund investing</a> explains the fund type the account uses.</p>'
            . '<h2>What it could grow to</h2><p>An illustration with the <a href="{tool:trump-account-calculator}">calculator</a>: if a child receives the $1,000 pilot deposit and the family adds $600 a year (about $50 a month), a 7% yearly return would give about $23,779 at age 18, from $11,800 of contributions. At 4% it would be about $17,413 and at 10% about $32,919. Left alone until age 65 at 7%, the age-18 balance would grow to roughly $571,791. These are illustrations, not forecasts. Returns vary every year, can be negative, and fund fees would reduce the result.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump baby account: children born 2025 to 2028</h2><p>Parents of newborns often search for a "Trump baby account" or "Trump kids account". Babies and young children born from January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028 are the group that can receive the one-time $1,000 federal deposit, so for these families the first step is making the election. Children born earlier, or later than 2028, can still have a Trump account, but without the $1,000 from the government.</p>'
            . '<h2>Trump account for older kids</h2><p>A Trump account for older kids is possible: the IRS describes accounts for children who have not turned 18 by the end of the election year, so a child born before 2025 can have one. The $1,000 pilot deposit does not apply to them, so their account starts with whatever the family, relatives or an employer contribute, up to the $5,000 yearly limit.</p>'
            . '<h2>Is the Trump account a savings account for kids?</h2><p>It is closer to a long-term investment account than a bank savings account. The money is invested in a U.S. stock index fund, so the balance can go up and down, and it is generally locked until the year the child turns 18. For short-term goals, a regular savings account is a better fit. A Trump child savings account, as people often call it, is meant for the long run.</p>'
            . '<h2>Questions parents ask</h2><ul><li><strong>My child was born in 2024. Can they have an account?</strong> The IRS describes accounts for children who have not turned 18 by the end of the election year, but the $1,000 federal deposit is limited to children born 2025 through 2028. Older and younger children can still have an account without the pilot deposit.</li><li><strong>Can grandparents or friends contribute?</strong> Yes. Contributions from family and others count toward the same $5,000 yearly limit.</li><li><strong>Does an employer contribution count toward the limit?</strong> Yes. An employer can contribute up to $2,500 per employee, and that amount counts toward the $5,000.</li><li><strong>Do I need a Social Security number?</strong> Yes. The child needs one, and the pilot deposit also requires U.S. citizenship.</li><li><strong>Can I pick any investment?</strong> No. The account must hold an index fund that tracks the S&amp;P 500 or a similar index of mainly U.S. stocks.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What we know so far: a short timeline</h2><ul><li><strong>2025:</strong> Congress creates Trump Accounts in the One Big Beautiful Bill Act.</li><li><strong>December 2, 2025:</strong> the IRS and Treasury publish guidance (IR-2025-117 and Notice 2025-68) and announce that regulations are coming. Form 4547 is released for making elections.</li><li><strong>July 4, 2026:</strong> contributions to Trump Accounts can begin.</li><li><strong>Ahead:</strong> final regulations, trustee announcements and employer programs. Fees and details can change.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Pros and cons</h2><h3>Potential advantages</h3><ul><li>A free $1,000 for eligible children</li><li>Long time horizon for compound growth</li><li>Low-cost index investing is built in</li><li>Employers can add up to $2,500 tax-free</li></ul><h3>Things to weigh</h3><ul><li>Money is locked up until the child is 18, and treated like an IRA after that</li><li>Investments are limited to U.S. stock index funds, so there is no diversification into bonds</li><li>Regulations are not final</li><li>It is a long-term account, so it does not replace an emergency fund or a 529 for education</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li>Assuming every child gets the $1,000. It applies to eligible children born 2025 to 2028.</li><li>Missing the election. The pilot deposit needs an election to be made.</li><li>Contributing more than $5,000 in a year across all sources.</li><li>Treating the account as a college fund. Use a 529 for education costs.</li><li>Trusting unofficial offers. Use only IRS.gov and trumpaccounts.gov to enroll, and never pay a fee to someone who claims they can "unlock" your child’s $1,000.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not tax or investment advice. Rules may change as the IRS and Treasury finalize regulations.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'    => [
            [ 'What is the Trump account for kids?', 'A new type of individual retirement account for eligible children, created by the 2025 tax law. It must be invested in U.S. stock index funds, and children born 2025 to 2028 can receive a one-time $1,000 federal deposit.' ],
            [ 'Is there a Trump account for older kids?', 'Yes. Children who have not turned 18 by the end of the election year can have an account, but only children born from 2025 through 2028 qualify for the $1,000 federal deposit.' ],
            [ 'Is there a Trump baby account?', 'Newborns and young children born 2025 to 2028 can have a Trump account and receive the $1,000 federal deposit once an election is made for them.' ],
            [ 'Who is eligible for a Trump account?', 'A child under 18 with a Social Security number. For the $1,000 deposit the child must also be a U.S. citizen born from 2025 through 2028.' ],
            [ 'Who gets the $1,000 from the government?', 'U.S. citizen children born from January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028 who have a Social Security number and for whom an election is made.' ],
            [ 'How much can be contributed each year?', 'Up to $5,000 in total from family, friends and employers for 2026 and 2027, with employer contributions of up to $2,500 counting toward that limit. The $1,000 pilot deposit is outside the limit.' ],
            [ 'When can the money be withdrawn?', 'Under IRS guidance, generally not before January 1 of the year the child turns 18. After that, the account is generally treated like a traditional IRA.' ],
            [ 'How do I open a Trump account for my child?', 'File IRS Form 4547 with a tax return, or use the online election at trumpaccounts.gov, and follow the official instructions.' ],
            [ 'What about the Trump account $250?', 'Michael and Susan Dell have publicly pledged $250 for certain younger children who do not qualify for the federal $1,000. Check trumpaccounts.gov for the current terms.' ],
            [ 'Is a Trump Account better than a 529?', 'They serve different goals. A 529 plan is built for education costs; a Trump Account is a long-term IRA-style account. Some families may use both.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
